**Code Explanation:**

The script performs the first step of the analysis by computing global Lagrangian statistics from the provided tracer trajectories. 1. Unrolling Trajectories: The periodic trajectories (domain [0, 2\pi]) are unrolled into continuous absolute positions. Any jump larger than \pi between consecutive time steps is identified as a periodic boundary crossing and corrected by adding or subtracting 2\pi. 2. Mean Squared Displacement (MSD): The MSD is computed for a logarithmically spaced set of time lags \tau. The anomalous diffusion exponent H is estimated by performing a log-log linear fit of the MSD versus \tau in the intermediate asymptotic regime (\tau \in [10, 100]). 3. Stable Index \alpha(\tau): For each lag \tau, the characteristic function of the displacements \phi(k) = \langle \cos(k \Delta x) \rangle is computed. By fitting -\ln(\phi(k)) to |D k|^\alpha in log-log space for a suitable range of k, the stable index \alpha(\tau) is estimated. This captures the transition from Gaussian (\alpha \approx 2) to heavy-tailed (\alpha < 2) behavior. 4. Tail Exponent: To cross-validate the characteristic function fit, the far tail (top 5%) of the displacement distribution is fitted to a power law using a log-log fit of the histogram, yielding an alternative estimate of the stable index \alpha_{tail}. 5. Lagrangian Velocity Autocorrelation: Lagrangian velocities are computed using finite differences of the unrolled trajectories. The velocity autocorrelation function R_v(\tau) is then calculated, and its decorrelation time is estimated by integrating R_v(\tau) up to its first zero-crossing. 6. Data Saving: All computed metrics, including unrolled trajectories, velocities, MSD, \alpha(\tau), R_v(\tau), and representative displacement PDFs, are saved to the data/ directory for use in subsequent steps.

